* Division, done by a snippet somebody else wrote.
*
* Everything else in `programs/macros/` is built out of `@to` and named cells,
* which is what the macro language is for. This one is not: `lib/fast.bfm`
* pastes in the divmod from the esolangs catalogue verbatim, pins the cells it
* needs beside each other, and tells the expander where the cursor lands --
* because the algorithm is a pointer walking a fixed workspace, and naming
* cells is exactly what it does not do.
*
* It costs about a third of what the same division costs built out of named
* idioms, and it comes with two holes the wiki does not mention: a divisor of
* one walks it off the end of its workspace, and a divisor of zero has no
* answer. Both are answered in the macro, which is the useful half of wrapping
* a snippet rather than pasting it.
*
* `@divmod` cannot yet appear inside a loop -- see `lib/fast.bfm` for why --
* so this program is a straight line, which is also why it can be read.

@include "lib/fast.bfm"

@var n
@var d
@var q
@var r
@var out

* One division, printed as `n/d=q,r`. Single digits, so the printing is not
* the point.
@macro show(nn, dd) {
    @put(n, nn)
    @put(d, dd)
    @divmod(n, d, q, r)
    @show_digit(n)
    @say(out, '/')
    @show_digit(d)
    @say(out, '=')
    @show_digit(q)
    @say(out, ',')
    @show_digit(r)
    @say(out, SPACE)
}

@show(9, 2)
@show(7, 7)
@show(5, 9)
@show(6, 3)
@show(0, 4)

* The two the snippet cannot do.
@show(8, 1)
@show(4, 0)
@newline(out)
